#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# interval_member_q

#% text
# `interval_member_q(i, x)` gives `true` if the number `x` lies in the interval `i`, or every piece of the interval `x` lies in `i`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `i` is an `interval(...)` of real pieces or a `centered_interval(c, r)`; a complex centre and radius give a rectangle in the complex plane.

#% item
# `interval_member_q(i)` is a function that tests membership.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
interval_member_q(interval([2, 5]), pi)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
interval_member_q(interval([2, 5]), interval([3, 4]))
#% output
#= true

#% cell
interval_member_q(interval([2, 5]), interval([4, 6]))
#% output
#= false

#% cell
interval_member_q(centered_interval(2 + 3*im, 1 + im), e + pi*im)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
interval_member_q(interval([3, 4]))(pi)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `interval`, `centered_interval`, `interval_union`
